5. Recommended Migration Approach

Phase 1: Bronze materialization

Create native Delta copies of all foreign catalog tables.

Example:

CREATE OR REPLACE TABLE target_catalog.bronze_schema.table_name
AS
SELECT *
FROM foreign_catalog.source_schema.table_name;

Why Bronze first
	•	simplest path to bring data into Databricks
	•	preserves source-like raw state
	•	separates ingestion from optimization
	•	enables faster retries and validation
	•	avoids mixing raw landing and business optimization in one step

Important note

CTAS into Bronze is fine for initial materialization, but it does not automatically produce the best layout for analytical performance.

That is why Bronze should be treated as:
	•	raw/native copy
	•	minimal transformation layer
	•	not necessarily the final query layer

⸻

6. Why Bronze Alone Is Not Enough

If all 6,000 tables are only copied using CTAS, then:
	•	large tables may not be partitioned appropriately
	•	some tables may have too many files or poorly distributed files
	•	full table scans may still be expensive
	•	frequently used analytical workloads may remain slower than desired

So the best practice is not:

Foreign catalog → one CTAS → final production table

The best practice is:

Foreign catalog → Bronze → Silver optimized tables

⸻

7. Best Practice Design: Bronze and Silver

Bronze

Purpose:
	•	raw historical landing
	•	direct materialization from source
	•	minimal logic
	•	easier reconciliation with SQL Server

Recommended characteristics:
	•	schema close to source
	•	audit columns if needed
	•	avoid unnecessary partitioning
	•	keep as simple as possible

Silver

Purpose:
	•	query-optimized version
	•	cleaned and standardized
	•	partitioned only where justified
	•	consumed by downstream reporting and PySpark

Recommended characteristics:
	•	partition large tables on appropriate business/date columns
	•	apply repartitioning before write when needed
	•	standardize datatypes if required
	•	optimize file layout

⸻

8. Partitioning Guidance

Partitioning every table is not a best practice.

Partition only if:
	•	table is large
	•	common filters are on a low-to-moderate cardinality column
	•	examples: service_month, load_date, year_month, state, market, line_of_business

Do not partition if:
	•	table is small
	•	column has very high cardinality
	•	partitioning would create too many small files
	•	the table is rarely queried

Recommended rule
	•	Small tables: no partitioning
	•	Medium tables: evaluate based on query pattern
	•	Large tables: partition only on columns actually used in filters

