collect(): Retrieves the entire dataset to the driver, potentially causing memory issues for large data.

- The collect() action retrieves the entire DataFrame or RDD and brings it to the driver program as a list.
- It’s useful when you need to access the complete dataset on the driver node, but be careful! For very large datasets, this can cause memory issues, as the entire dataset will be loaded into the driver’s memory.
- 

take(): Efficiently retrieves a specified number of rows without loading the whole dataset into memory.

- The take() action is used to retrieve a specified number of records from the DataFrame or RDD without bringing the entire dataset to the driver.
- This makes it more memory-efficient compared to collect() for large datasets.

cache vs persist:

Someone might think that why can't cache or persisted all the data frames are all the transformations. That is possible, but it will consume the space in the memory. we have fixed memory that is ram and we need to have space for storing the stuff in memory, as well as for computation and that is why we can't do it for all the transformations

cache - always in memory

persist - in memory or disk storage or both

